    color: var(--text-faint);
    max-width: 1160px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="report-header">
  <div class="eyebrow">Energy Markets &mdash; Commodity Strategy</div>
  <h1>Global Crude Oil Price Trends:<br>A Historical Analysis (1970&ndash;2026)</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis of monthly crude oil prices spanning over five decades, identifying key price regimes, major shocks, and long-term structural patterns in the global energy market.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <div>Dataset: <span>675 monthly observations &bull; Jan 1970 &ndash; Mar 2026</span></div>
    <div>Source: <span>Kaggle &mdash; Global Fuel Price Trends (1970&ndash;2026)</span></div>
    <div>Currency: <span>Nominal USD per barrel</span></div>
    <div>Published: <span>April 2026</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

  <!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">01</span>
      <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card accent">
        <div class="kpi-label">All-Time High</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$132.83</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">July 2008 &bull; Pre-GFC peak</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">All-Time Low</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$1.21</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">All of 1970 &bull; Pre-embargo era</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card green">
        <div class="kpi-label">CAGR (1970&ndash;2026)</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">7.93%</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Nominal USD &bull; 56.2 years</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card gold">
        <div class="kpi-label">Overall Price Range</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$131.62</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Max minus Min &bull; Extreme dispersion</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Std Deviation</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$29.25</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">74.2% of the mean ($39.44)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="kpi-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(4,1fr);">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Mean Price</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$39.44</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Across 675 months</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Median Price</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$29.10</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">50th percentile &bull; Right-skewed dist.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card gold">
        <div class="kpi-label">Highest-Price Decade</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">2010s</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Avg $72.39/bbl &bull; 120 months</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card red">
        <div class="kpi-label">Largest MoM Drop</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">&minus;$26.97</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Oct 2008 &bull; GFC crash</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="prose">
      Over the 56-year span from January 1970 through March 2026, crude oil prices have undergone transformations that mirror the most consequential geopolitical and macroeconomic events of the modern era. Starting at a nominal <strong>$1.21/bbl</strong>, prices multiplied more than 100-fold to peak at <strong>$132.83/bbl</strong> in July 2008. The compound annual growth rate of <strong>7.93%</strong> in nominal terms significantly outpaces many asset classes over the same period, though this figure obscures long stretches of stagnation, collapse, and recovery.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The distribution of monthly prices is <strong>strongly right-skewed</strong>: the mean ($39.44) sits well above the median ($29.10), reflecting the outsized weight of the high-price regimes of the 2000s and 2010s. Four major structural regimes are identifiable: (1) the pre-embargo low-price era of the early 1970s; (2) the OPEC shock period and aftermath (1973–1986); (3) the post-glut consolidation and relative stability of the 1990s; and (4) the supercycle and subsequent volatility from 2000 onward. Each regime shift was precipitated by discrete supply, demand, or geopolitical shocks—and each fundamentally reset the market's perception of a "normal" price.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 2: LONG-TERM TREND -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">02</span>
      <h2>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970&ndash;2026)</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <div class="chart-title">Monthly Crude Oil Price &mdash; Full Time Series (1970&ndash;2026)</div>
      <div class="chart-desc">Each point represents one monthly observation. Annotated inflection points correspond to major geopolitical or economic events. Prices in nominal USD/bbl.</div>
      <canvas id="mainChart" height="320"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <div class="chart-title">Annual Average Crude Oil Price (1970&ndash;2026)</div>
      <div class="chart-desc">Year-level averages smooth intra-year volatility and reveal structural regime shifts more clearly.</div>
      <canvas id="annualChart" height="240"></canvas>
    </div>

    <p class="prose">
      The full time series reveals five visually distinct price regimes. <strong>1970–1972</strong> saw prices stable below $2/bbl under the regulated Bretton Woods-era system. The <strong>1973 OPEC embargo</strong> triggered the most violent single-step repricing in the dataset—a 217% month-over-month jump from $4.10 to $13.00 in January 1974. A secondary supply shock (Iranian Revolution, Iran-Iraq War) drove prices further to <strong>$40.75/bbl by November 1979</strong>. The <strong>1986 Saudi production surge</strong> unwound a decade of high prices in a matter of months, with prices bottoming at <strong>$9.62/bbl in July 1986</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The 1990s represented a prolonged consolidation phase—prices ranged between roughly $10 and $25/bbl for most of the decade, broken briefly by the Gulf War spike (October 1990: $34.50) and the Asian financial crisis collapse (December 1998: $10.41). The <strong>2000s supercycle</strong> was the most relentless structural bull market in the dataset, driven by Chinese demand growth, constrained spare capacity, and speculative flows—peaking at <strong>$132.83 in July 2008</strong> before the Global Financial Crisis erased 69% of value in five months. Post-GFC recovery was rapid; the 2010s settled into a <strong>high plateau averaging $72.39/bbl</strong> before the shale revolution and OPEC price war precipitated a 2014–2016 collapse. The COVID-19 demand shock of <strong>April 2020 ($21.04/bbl)</strong> was the most severe since the 1980s glut. The subsequent recovery reached $116.80 in June 2022, driven by post-pandemic demand normalization and Russia-Ukraine supply disruption.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 3: DECADE BY DECADE -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">03</span>
      <h2>Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <div class="chart-title">Average Price by Decade</div>
        <div class="chart-desc">Mean monthly price for each complete or near-complete decade.</div>
        <canvas id="decadeAvgChart" height="280"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <div class="chart-title">Price Volatility by Decade (Std Deviation)</div>
        <div class="chart-desc">Standard deviation of monthly prices within each decade.</div>
        <canvas id="decadeVolChart" height="280"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Decade</th>
            <th class="td-num">Avg Price</th>
            <th class="td-num">Std Dev</th>
            <th class="td-num">Min</th>
            <th class="td-num">Max</th>
            <th class="td-num">Months</th>
            <th>Defining Characteristics</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1970s</strong></td>
            <td class="td-num">$9.75</td>
            <td class="td-num">$8.91</td>
            <td class="td-num">$1.21 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Jan 1970)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">$40.75 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Nov 1979)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">120</td>
            <td>OPEC embargo (1973), Iranian Revolution (1979) — decade ended ~33× above start</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1980s</strong></td>
            <td class="td-num">$25.51</td>
            <td class="td-num">$7.98</td>
            <td class="td-num">$9.62 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Jul 1986)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">$40.30 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Nov 1980)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">120</td>
            <td>High price inherited from 1970s shocks; Saudi production surge (1986) caused 76% intra-decade crash</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1990s</strong></td>
            <td class="td-num">$19.16</td>
            <td class="td-num">$3.89</td>
            <td class="td-num">$10.41 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Dec 1998)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">$34.50 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Oct 1990)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">120</td>
            <td>Lowest volatility decade — Gulf War spike quickly reversed; Asian crisis 1997–98 depressed prices</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2000s</strong></td>
            <td class="td-num">$50.57</td>
            <td class="td-num">$25.41</td>
            <td class="td-num">$18.52 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Nov 2001)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">$132.83 <span style="color:var(--text-faint)">(Jul 2008)</span></td>
            <td class="td-num">120</td>
            <td>China-driven supercycle; highest dispersion decade. GFC crash wiped 69% peak-to-trough in 5 months</td>
