    <p class="prose">
      <strong>The five most severe shock events</strong> in the dataset, ranked by peak month-over-month magnitude:
    </p>

    <ul class="shock-list">
      <li>
        <div class="shock-date">Jan 1974</div>
        <div class="shock-text">
          <strong>OPEC Arab Oil Embargo (1973–74) <span class="badge badge-up">+217%</span></strong>
          Price jumped from $4.10 (Dec 1973) to $13.00 (Jan 1974) — a 217% single-month repricing. On a year-over-year basis, January 1974 was +525% above January 1973. The embargo, imposed in October 1973 during the Yom Kippur War, created the first global oil supply crisis of the modern era and permanently reset price expectations.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="shock-date">Nov 1979 &ndash; Feb 1980</div>
        <div class="shock-text">
          <strong>Iranian Revolution / Iran-Iraq War <span class="badge badge-up">+$40.75 peak</span></strong>
          The second OPEC shock drove prices from $14.50 (Dec 1978) to $40.75 (Nov 1979) — a 181% rise over 11 months. The Iran-Iraq War (starting Sep 1980) kept prices elevated above $35/bbl through 1981. This period marked the dataset's second-highest price level before the 2000s supercycle.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="shock-date">Feb &ndash; Jul 1986</div>
        <div class="shock-text">
          <strong>Saudi Arabia Price War / OPEC Glut <span class="badge badge-dn">&minus;61%</span></strong>
          Saudi Arabia abandoned its swing producer role in late 1985 and ramped production, triggering a collapse from $26.50 (Dec 1985) to $9.62 (Jul 1986) — a 64% fall in seven months. February 1986 saw the second-largest negative MoM change in the dataset (&minus;35.5%). The crash ended a decade of high prices and permanently damaged OPEC cohesion for years.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="shock-date">Jul &ndash; Dec 2008</div>
        <div class="shock-text">
          <strong>Global Financial Crisis <span class="badge badge-dn">&minus;$91.49 drawdown</span></strong>
          From the all-time high of $132.83 (Jul 2008), prices collapsed to $41.34 (Dec 2008) — a 69% drawdown in five months. October 2008 alone fell $26.97 (&minus;27%), the largest single-month absolute drop in the dataset. The GFC-driven demand destruction was the fastest sustained collapse since 1986 and occurred from the dataset's highest price level.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="shock-date">Mar &ndash; Apr 2020</div>
        <div class="shock-text">
          <strong>COVID-19 Demand Shock <span class="badge badge-dn">&minus;39.6% in Mar; &minus;34.7% in Apr</span></strong>
          The dual shock of pandemic demand destruction and OPEC+ price war drove the fastest consecutive monthly decline in the dataset. March 2020: &minus;39.6% ($53.35→$32.20). April 2020: &minus;34.7% ($32.20→$21.04). The April 2020 price of $21.04 was the lowest since December 2001. The YoY change at April 2020 was &minus;67.1%, the steepest in the entire dataset.
        </div>
      </li>
