  <!-- SECTION 6: OUTLOOK -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">06</span>
      <h2>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <div class="chart-title">2020–2026 Detailed View</div>
      <div class="chart-desc">Monthly prices during the current decade, highlighting the COVID trough, Ukraine-driven peak, and current trajectory.</div>
      <canvas id="recentChart" height="220"></canvas>
    </div>

    <p class="prose">
      <strong>Current cycle context (2020–2026):</strong> The 2020s opened with the deepest demand shock in the dataset (April 2020: $21.04). The recovery was unusually sharp — by June 2021 prices exceeded $71, and by June 2022 had reached $116.80, driven by post-pandemic demand normalization and supply disruption following Russia's invasion of Ukraine. The subsequent 2022–2025 descent has been orderly rather than a crash — from $116.80 to $57.95 by December 2025, a 50% decline over 42 months, with OPEC+ production adjustments buffering deeper falls.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>The March 2026 spike to $87.84</strong> (+36.2% MoM from $64.47) represents the single largest positive month-over-month absolute change in the dataset ($+23.37). Whether this represents a new upswing or a transient supply disruption event cannot be determined from price data alone. Historical analogues suggest post-correction spikes of this magnitude have sometimes preceded sustained rallies (1999, 2009) and sometimes been one-month outliers that reverted quickly (mid-2000s volatility). Analysts should monitor supply-side fundamentals and OPEC+ compliance closely.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>Structural conclusions from 56 years of data:</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      (1) <strong>CAGR of 7.93% in nominal terms</strong> overstates real returns — inflation-adjusted prices in the $40–80 range would be considerably lower for the early decades. The absence of CPI deflation in this dataset means real price comparisons across decades require additional data.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      (2) <strong>Recovery velocity has accelerated.</strong> The 1986 crash took 15 years to fully reverse. The 2008 crash was 80% recovered within 2 years. The 2020 COVID trough was 60% recovered within 8 months. The market's structural mean-reversion speed has increased, possibly reflecting more reactive OPEC+ policy, financial market participation in oil futures, and faster global demand recovery capacity.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      (3) <strong>The 2010s average ($72.39/bbl) likely represents the new structural floor</strong> for a supply-constrained market assuming global demand remains near current levels. Prices consistently below $50 have historically required major demand destruction (2020 lockdowns) or deliberate supply flooding (1986, 2014–2016).
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      (4) <strong>Tail risk remains asymmetric to the upside.</strong> The all-time high of $132.83 was achieved in a period of constrained spare capacity and surging demand. If those conditions recur — reduced capital expenditure in upstream oil, accelerating demand from developing economies — a retest of the 2008 highs cannot be excluded. Conversely, a structural demand peak scenario driven by energy transition would represent a regime shift without clear historical precedent in this dataset.
    </p>
  </div>

